    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The novel draws mainly on Chen Shou's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Records of the Three Kingdoms</a>, but adds fictional scenes, speeches and supernatural episodes, and gives its moral weight to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> and Shu Han.</li><li>Its best-known opening line, on the empire long divided and long united, was added by Mao Lun and Mao Zonggang in their recension, and none of the earlier editions contained it.</li><li>Translated first into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> in 1650 and later into many languages, it is regarded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> as a canonical work and has been called the national novel of Korea.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a>'s southern campaign, which Chen Shou described in a few sentences as a single action, became seven battles in the Mao edition, in which the rebel leader Meng Huo is captured and released seven times.</p></aside>
    </section>

